# Badges C–D: checked against official scouting.org excerpts.

@owner mb.collections
@complete complete
@note Stamp and coin collecting are not eligible for this badge; they have their own badges.
@desc Collections is for Scouts with a collecting hobby: describing and growing your collection, preserving, displaying and cataloging it, collecting versus investing, and the terms, grading and organizations of your hobby.
1 a Your collection :: A short written description of your collection, its history, why you enjoy it and what you have learned.
2 k Growth of your collection :: How your collection has grown and developed.
3 demo Preserving and cataloging :: Preserving and displaying your collection, events for collectors, and your cataloging method.
4 k Collecting and investing :: How investing and speculation relate to your kind of collection.
5 m Terms, grading and value :: Terms used in your hobby, showing organized groups from your collection, grading and value, and collector associations.

@owner mb.composite-materials
@complete complete
@note The 2026 update changed requirement 6 to a careers-or-hobby choice.
@desc Composite Materials explores materials made by combining fibers and resins: how they compare with wood and metals, how they are made, two hands-on projects, and careers or hobbies that use them.
@safety Resins and fibers can be hazardous: work under qualified adult supervision, outdoors or with good ventilation, using the precautions on each safety data sheet.
1 k! Safety :: Likely hazards, protective equipment, handling and disposing of resins, and safety data sheets.
2 k What composites are :: What composite materials are and how they compare with wood and metals.
3 k Materials and manufacturing :: How composites are made, and several reinforcement materials and resins with their uses.
4 a Outside research :: One option: visiting a company that makes or repairs composites, or reviewing composites websites.
5 a! Composite projects :: Two projects using composite materials, at least one from the pamphlet.
6 a Careers or hobbies :: One option: a career related to composites, or a hobby that uses these skills.

@owner mb.crime-prevention
@complete complete
@desc Crime Prevention looks at why laws exist and how communities keep people safe: crime in your area, security surveys, retail crime, reporting crime and abuse, peer pressure, substance use, online safety, and careers in public safety.
1 d Crime and the law :: Why criminal laws exist, types of crime, why people commit crimes, and what crime prevention means.
2 k Who prevents crime :: How citizens, schools and neighborhood or youth groups help prevent crime.
3 a Crime in your community :: Researching news coverage of several different kinds of crime.
4 a Security survey and lesson :: How design and community groups deter crime, a home or building security survey, and teaching a lesson.
5 k Retail crime :: The effects of shoplifting and employee theft, and how stores prevent them.
6 k! Reporting crime and staying safe :: When and how to report crimes, warning signs of abuse, and personal safety rules.
7 d Peers and crime :: Peer pressure, bullying, hazing and gangs.
8 k Substance use and crime :: Legal and health consequences of substances, their link to crime, and where to get help.
9 k Online crime :: Scams, identity theft, online security, and how bullying and sharing images can become crimes.
10 a Interview :: Interviewing a law enforcement officer or other public servant about crime prevention.
11 a Careers :: Careers in crime prevention and researching one of them.

@owner mb.cybersecurity
@complete partial
@note The excerpt was cut off partway through requirement 6; any later requirements are not outlined yet.
@desc Cybersecurity teaches you to protect yourself and your devices: online safety and ethics, what attackers target, common threats like malware and phishing, practical defenses such as strong passwords, multi-factor authentication and backups, and the basics of cryptography.
1 k Safety :: Your digital footprint, privacy settings and the physical effects of device use.
2 d Ethics :: Applying the Scout Law online and handling situations such as an unlocked device or exposed password.
3 k Attack surface :: The systems and information that need protecting and why.
4 k Threats and attacks :: Vulnerabilities, threats and exploits, malware, public Wi-Fi risks, and phishing and spoofing.
5 demo Cyber defenses :: Defensive technologies and hands-on steps such as strong passwords, multi-factor authentication, updates, scans and backups.
6 a Cryptography :: Encryption, checking for an encrypted connection, and making or using a cipher.

@owner mb.dentistry
@complete complete
@desc Dentistry explores how teeth work and how to keep them healthy: tooth structure and X-rays, decay and gum disease, a dental visit, fluoride and whole-body health, hands-on projects, and careers in dentistry.
1 a Teeth and X-rays :: Studying dental X-rays and drawing and labeling a tooth.
2 k Decay and gum disease :: Causes of tooth decay and gum disease, mouth guards, and the effects of tobacco.
3 a Visiting a dentist :: Visiting a dental office and learning how the team works.
4 a Dental options :: Two options, such as identifying dental instruments or tracking your diet.
5 k Fluoride and health :: How fluoride helps teeth and how oral health connects to overall health.
6 a Dental projects :: Two projects, such as a model tooth, a poster or an article.
7 a Dental careers :: Researching dental careers and exploring one in depth.

@owner mb.digital-technology
@complete complete
@desc Digital Technology explains how digital devices, software and the internet work: digital safety and the Cyber Chip, compression and networks, apps and malware, productivity tools, digital projects for your troop, intellectual property, e-waste, and careers.
1 k Digital safety :: The Personal Safety Awareness “Digital Safety” video and the Cyber Chip.
2 d History and future :: How digital technology has changed over time and may change in the future.
3 k How devices work :: Digitizing media, compression, programming, and local and wide area networks.
4 k Software and security :: How apps run, malware and protecting against it, and barcodes, QR codes and RFID.
5 a The internet and productivity :: How data moves online, secure connections, and using productivity software for troop tasks.
6 a Digital projects :: Projects for your troop such as photos, audio recordings and a blog.
7 k Intellectual property :: Copyright, patents, trademarks and trade secrets, and using software legally.
8 a Disposal and recycling :: Options on safely disposing of and recycling electronics and batteries.
9 a Careers :: One option: researching a career or visiting a facility that uses digital technology.

@owner mb.disabilities-awareness
@complete complete
@desc Disabilities Awareness builds understanding and respect: disability etiquette and terms, visiting an agency, meeting people with disabilities, accessibility and accommodation, outreach, a personal commitment, and careers serving people with disabilities.
1 k Terms and etiquette :: Disability terms such as accessibility, accommodation and person-first language, and why etiquette matters.
2 a Visiting an agency :: Visiting an agency that works with people with disabilities and learning about its work.
3 a Learning from others :: Two options, such as talking with a person with a disability, an adaptive sport, independent-living aids or an awareness activity.
4 a Accessibility or accommodation :: One option: surveying locations for accessibility, or for accommodations for invisible disabilities.
5 s Outreach :: One option: a presentation, volunteer service, or researching common myths about disability.
6 d Your commitment :: A commitment to a positive attitude toward people with disabilities, and how your awareness has changed.
7 a Careers :: Professions that serve people with disabilities and the training for one of them.

@owner mb.dog-care
@note Excerpts showed the topics (history and breeds, body parts, training and responsibilities, a two-month care log, health, first aid, a veterinarian or shelter visit, dog laws, and careers or hobbies) but not their numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Dog Care teaches responsible dog ownership: breeds and history, how a dog’s body works, feeding, grooming and training, keeping a care log, preventive health and first aid, local dog laws, and careers working with dogs.
@safety Approach unfamiliar dogs only with the owner’s permission; dog bites need prompt first aid and medical attention.

@owner mb.drafting
@complete partial
@note Excerpts were partly out of order; requirements 5–7 are outlined from topic and should be confirmed against the official list.
@desc Drafting teaches technical drawing by hand and on computer: preparing drawing sheets, scale drawings with a bill of materials, a CAD drawing, comparing manual and computer drafting, lettering, visiting a workplace, and how drafting tools have changed.
1 a Preparing drawing sheets :: Borders, title blocks and rough sketches for your drawing projects.
2 a A scale drawing :: An architectural, mechanical or electrical drawing with a bill of materials.
3 a A CAD drawing :: A computer-aided drawing with its title block completed.
4 d Manual versus CAD :: Comparing your manual and computer drafting work.
5 a Lettering :: A short explanation of a benefit of CAD, lettered by hand.
6 a Drafting at work :: Visiting a workplace where drafting is used, or a similar investigation.
7 k Tools then and now :: How drafting tools and media have changed, including the effect of CAD.

@owner mb.wildland-fire-management
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/skills/merit-badges/digital-resource-guides/wildland-fire-management/contents/ | Wildland Fire Management Digital Resource Guide (scouting.org)
@note This badge was added in 2026 and uses a digital resource guide instead of a printed pamphlet.
@desc Wildland Fire Management covers the history of fighting and managing wildfire, its harms and benefits, prescribed fire, prevention, fire behavior, firefighter safety and tactics, hand tools, a hands-on fire readiness option, a major historical fire, and careers.
@safety Never start or approach a wildland fire. Tool demonstrations and field visits happen only with qualified fire professionals.
1 k History :: The history of wildland fire suppression, prevention and management in the United States.
2 k Harms of wildfire :: Effects on forest products, wildlife habitat, water, recreation, homes and air quality.
3 k Benefits and prescribed fire :: How prescribed fire reduces fuel and restores ecosystems.
4 k Prevention :: Causes of wildfire and the wildland–urban interface.
5 k Fire behavior :: How weather, topography and fuel affect fire, and the parts of a wildfire.
6 k! Firefighter safety :: Protective equipment, the standard firefighting orders, watch-out situations and LCES.
7 k Suppression tactics :: Direct and indirect attack and the Incident Command System.
8 demo! Wildland tools :: Hand tools used in wildland firefighting and other firefighting equipment.
9 a Fire readiness :: One option, such as a fire readiness plan, a Firewise assessment, or meeting a forestry, prevention or fire-weather professional.
10 a A historic wildfire :: A catastrophic U.S. wildfire and how it changed policy.
11 a Careers :: Researching a career in wildland fire.

@owner mb.competitive-gaming
@note Competitive Gaming is a Test Lab badge. Its official page and digital guide group the requirements into history of gaming, gamer safety, health and balance, community connection, technology, leadership and service, and careers, but the two sources worded some items differently, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Competitive Gaming (a Test Lab badge) looks at esports and gaming responsibly: gaming history, online safety, balancing gaming with sleep and physical activity, the technology behind competitive play, leadership and service in the gaming community, and careers in the industry.

@owner mb.military-service-and-veterans
@note This is a Test Lab badge; its requirements were not visible in the official excerpts, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Military Service & Veterans (a Test Lab badge) invites Scouts to explore the values, history and human stories behind military service, including the experiences of veterans in their own communities.
